182 0 obj
<<
/Title (Password hashing: User passwords are not stored in plain text. The application uses bcrypt to hash passwords before saving them in the database.  Server-side validation: Usernames and passwords are validated on the server, not only in the browser.  Notes are also limited to valid length.  SQL Injection protection: Database operations use parameterized queries with placeholders instead of  directly inserting user input into SQL statements.  XSS protection: The application uses Flask/Jinja autoescaping so that user-submitted note content is  displayed as text instead of executable HTML or JavaScript.  Login attempt limitation: The system locks an account temporarily after repeated failed login attempts  to reduce brute-force and dictionary attack risk.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 119 0 R
/Next 216 0 R
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Title (7. Conclusion  Thisprojectdemonstrated how a basic web application can be built and secured against common vulnerabilities. The testing showed that SQL Injection and XSS attacks can be prevented through parameterized queries and output escaping. The brute-force and dictionary attack tests showed the importance of login attempt limits and strong password policies. Overall, the project improved understanding of secure coding, ethical hacking, vulnerability testing, and professional security documentation.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 217 0 R
/Next 120 0 R
>>
endobj
