/Title (Expected result: The attack should fail because the application uses parameterized queries. The malicious input is treated as normal text rather than executable SQL code.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 216 0 R
/Next 265 0 R
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Title (Password hashing with bcrypt prevents attackers from seeing real passwords even if the database is exposed.  Parameterized queries prevent SQL Injection by separating SQL code from user input.  Server-side validation rejects invalid usernames, weak passwords, and overly long note content. Output escaping prevents stored XSS by making HTML and JavaScript payloads display as text.  Login attempt limitations reduce the effectiveness of brute-force and dictionary attacks by temporarily locking accounts after repeated failed attempts.  Strong password requirements reduce the chance of users choosing common or easily guessed  passwords.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 266 0 R
/Next 217 0 R
>>
endobj
